<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

All three take a required `cards` list and an optional `title`. An empty list is shown as a warning while you edit.

Gaps between cards are 8 pixels. The Civica theme does not change them. You can set `--stack-card-gap` for both stacks, or `--vertical-stack-card-gap`, `--horizontal-stack-card-gap` and `--grid-card-gap` for one kind, in a theme. For one card only, use card-mod, which needs the card-mod integration:

```yaml
type: vertical-stack
card_mod:
  style: |
    :host { --stack-card-gap: 16px; }
cards:
  - type: tile
    entity: light.living_room
```

The title uses the card heading colour, size and font that the Civica theme sets. Its alignment follows `--ha-stack-title-text-align`.

<h3 id="child-hui-grid-card"><code>hui-grid-card</code></h3>

Cards in equal columns (`type: grid`). Set `columns` for the number of columns; the default is 3. The count is fixed and does not change with the screen width.

By default each cell is square (`square: true`), which is good for button-like cards and wasteful for text. Set `square: false` so that each row is as tall as its content.

```yaml
type: grid
columns: 3
square: false
cards:
  - type: tile
    entity: light.lamp
  - type: tile
    entity: media_player.television
  - type: tile
    entity: cover.blinds
```

<h3 id="child-hui-horizontal-stack-card"><code>hui-horizontal-stack-card</code></h3>

Cards side by side in one row (`type: horizontal-stack`). Each card gets an equal share of the width. A horizontal stack does not wrap, so on a narrow screen every card gets narrower and text may be cut short or squeezed. Put up to three short button-like cards in a row, and two for anything with text.

<h3 id="child-hui-vertical-stack-card"><code>hui-vertical-stack-card</code></h3>

Cards one above another in one column (`type: vertical-stack`). It works at any width, so it is the safest choice for narrow screens. Putting a horizontal stack inside a vertical stack is common, but keep nesting shallow so that labels do not shrink.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A stack or grid keeps related cards together on any dashboard view.
- Spacing is the same everywhere and can be changed in one place.
- Neither the grid nor the horizontal stack wraps, so cards shrink on a narrow screen.
- A stack is not announced as a group, so do not rely on it to tell people which cards belong together. Give the title or the first card’s name that job.
- The stack title is drawn as a level 1 heading inside the card, which can clash with the heading levels on the page. Check it in your own dashboard.
- Nested stacks make reading order harder to predict.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Group cards that belong together and keep related controls close. Put up to three short button-like cards across, and two for anything with text. On a narrow screen, use a vertical stack or a sections view with grid options. Avoid nesting stacks more than one level. Put cards in the order you want them read.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 1.3.2 Meaningful Sequence: reading order follows the order of the list.
- 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.4 Resize Text: rows of cards squeeze on narrow screens and at large text sizes. Test at 320 CSS pixels in your own dashboard.
- 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: give the stack a clear title.

